{"units": [{"id": "emai-word3", "translation": "he had"}, {"id": "emai-word4", "translation": "he was"}, {"id": "emai-word5", "translation": "winds"}, {"id": "emar-orth1", "translation": "day"}, {"id": "emar-def1", "translation": "day"}, {"id": "emar-def2", "translation": "of the light of prosperity"}, {"id": "emar-note1", "translation": "κλαίοντες [weeping] Stanley"}, {"id": "emar-note2", "translation": "ἦμαρ [day] M"}, {"id": "emar-quote1", "translation": "to this day"}, {"id": "emar-quote2", "translation": "Why not lamenting, not obtaining a share of the day?"}, {"id": "emar-quote3", "translation": "a day of good omen"}, {"id": "emar-quote4", "translation": "throughout the bright day"}, {"id": "emar-quote5", "translation": "this day has come"}, {"id": "emar-quote6", "translation": "thinking to pass the day of slaughter cheerfully"}, {"id": "emar-quote7", "translation": "to the day ordained by fate"}, {"id": "emar-quote8", "translation": "both by day and likewise by night"}, {"id": "emar-quote9", "translation": "if I should recount ten days in order"}, {"id": "emar-quote10", "translation": "in days of ox-sacrifice"}, {"id": "emar-quote11", "translation": "bright day from black night"}, {"id": "emar-quote12", "translation": "a most beautiful day to behold after the storm"}, {"id": "emar-word1", "translation": "weeping"}, {"id": "emar-word2", "translation": "day M"}, {"id": "emees-orth1", "translation": "we"}, {"id": "emees-def1", "translation": "and the remaining cases, see under ἐγώ."}, {"id": "emees-word1", "translation": "I"}, {"id": "hemera-orth1", "translation": "day"}, {"id": "hemera-def1", "translation": "(ἁμέρα Doric) day"}, {"id": "hemera-note1", "translation": "in Stobaeus, Florilegium 98, 49"}, {"id": "hemera-note2", "translation": "The manuscripts have ἐφήμερα [ephemeral], except that in the Vienna manuscript and in Apostolius 20, 19 it is ἐφημέρια [daily]. ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρᾳ [for the day] Lobeck on Sophocles' Ajax p. 444."}, {"id": "hemera-quote1", "translation": "the white-horsed day has taken hold of all the earth"}, {"id": "hemera-quote2", "translation": "in one day"}, {"id": "hemera-quote3", "translation": "giving their lives for pleasure day by day"}, {"id": "hemera-quote4", "translation": "the Achaeans hold Troy on this day"}, {"id": "hemera-quote5", "translation": "O kindly light of the day that brings justice"}, {"id": "hemera-quote6", "translation": "and day by day nothing is more evident"}, {"id": "hemera-quote7", "translation": "and in the day the fate of mortals is unforeseen"}, {"id": "hemera-quote8", "translation": "in time and on the appointed day"}, {"id": "hemera-quote9", "translation": "never yet has a day without hunting sent Actaeon empty to his home"}, {"id": "hemera-quote10", "translation": "for the race of mortals thinks only for the day"}, {"id": "hemera-quote11", "translation": "a journey of twelve days"}, {"id": "hemera-quote12", "translation": "all the days"}, {"id": "hemera-quote13", "translation": "in later days"}, {"id": "hemera-word1", "translation": "day (Doric)"}, {"id": "hemera-word2", "translation": "ephemeral"}, {"id": "hemera-word3", "translation": "daily. for the day"}, {"id": "hemereuo-orth1", "translation": "to spend the day"}, {"id": "hemereuo-def1", "translation": "to spend the day"}, {"id": "hemereuo-note1", "translation": "where the genitive is governed by πρόσφορα [fitting things], with which Abresch compared Euripides Helen 515: τὰ πρόσφορα τῆς νῦν παρούσης ξυμφορᾶς αἰτήσομαι [I will ask for what is fitting for the present misfortune]."}, {"id": "hemereuo-quote1", "translation": "for strangers who have spent the day on a long journey to obtain what is fitting"}, {"id": "hemereuo-word1", "translation": "fitting things"}, {"id": "hemereuo-word2", "translation": "I will ask for what is fitting for the present misfortune"}, {"id": "hemeresios-orth1", "translation": "daily"}, {"id": "hemeresios-def1", "translation": "daily"}, {"id": "hemeresios-quote1", "translation": "daily light"}, {"id": "hemerolegdon-orth1", "translation": "counting day by day"}, {"id": "hemerolegdon-def1", "translation": "counting day by day"}, {"id": "hemerolegdon-note1", "translation": "The faulty accent in M, ἡμερόλεγδον [counting day by day], was corrected by Goettling."}, {"id": "hemerolegdon-quote1", "translation": "parents and wives tremble as they count the passing days"}, {"id": "hemerolegdon-word1", "translation": "counting day by day"}, {"id": "hemeros-orth1", "translation": "tame"}, {"id": "hemeros-def1", "translation": "(Doric ἅμερος) tame"}, {"id": "hemeros-quote1", "translation": "tame, loving children, and pleasing to the old"}, {"id": "hemeros-quote2", "translation": "but when conquered he will appear more gentle"}, {"id": "hemeros-word1", "translation": "tame (Doric)"}, {"id": "hemeroskopos-orth1", "translation": "watching by day"}, {"id": "hemeroskopos-def1", "translation": "watching by day"}, {"id": "hemeroskopos-quote1", "translation": "I will keep a faithful eye as a day-watcher"}, {"id": "hemerophantos-orth1", "translation": "appearing by day"}, {"id": "hemerophantos-def1", "translation": "appearing by day"}, {"id": "hemerophantos-quote1", "translation": "a dream appearing by day"}, {"id": "hemeroo-orth1", "translation": "to tame"}, {"id": "hemeroo-def1", "translation": "to tame"}, {"id": "hemeroo-quote1", "translation": "making the wild land tame"}, {"id": "emeteros-orth1", "translation": "our"}, {"id": "emeteros-def1", "translation": "(ἁμέτερος Doric) our"}, {"id": "emeteros-note1", "translation": "I wrote ἁμετέρας [our]"}, {"id": "emeteros-note2", "translation": "the words ἁμετέρᾳ πόλει [to our city] and at 162 ἁμετέρῳ στρατῷ [to our army] I added by conjecture."}, {"id": "emeteros-note3", "translation": "ἡμετέραις [our] M"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote1", "translation": "our just prayers"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote2", "translation": "our ancestral race"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote3", "translation": "with our black-robed approaches"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote4", "translation": "our strife"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote5", "translation": "our expedition"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote6", "translation": "our race"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote7", "translation": "our ancient race"}, {"id": "emeteros-quote8", "translation": "our father"}, {"id": "emeteros-word1", "translation": "our (Doric)"}, {"id": "emeteros-word2", "translation": "our"}, {"id": "emeteros-word3", "translation": "to our city"}, {"id": "emeteros-word4", "translation": "to our army"}, {"id": "emeteros-word5", "translation": "our M"}, {"id": "hemiopos-orth1", "translation": "small"}, {"id": "hemiopos-def1", "translation": "small"}, {"id": "hemiopos-note1", "translation": "καὶ τὸν ἐλάσσονα [and the smaller] Bothius deletes. καὶ τὸν ἐλάσσὼ [and the smaller] Porson"}, {"id": "hemiopos-quote1", "translation": "the great one quickly swallows the small and the lesser"}, {"id": "hemiopos-word1", "translation": "and the smaller"}, {"id": "hemiopos-word2", "translation": "and the smaller"}, {"id": "hemisys-orth1", "translation": "half"}, {"id": "hemisys-def1", "translation": "half"}, {"id": "hemisys-note1", "translation": "Thus from the copy, for λόγου [word]."}, {"id": "hemisys-quote1", "translation": "when two are present, half the word is there"}, {"id": "hemisys-word1", "translation": "word"}, {"id": "emoris-orth1", "translation": "empty"}, {"id": "emoris-def1", "translation": "empty"}, {"id": "emoris-note1", "translation": "κενὴ, ἐστερημένη [empty, deprived]. Aeschylus in Niobe (fr. 162). ἤμορος [bereft] is explained by ἄμοιρος [without share] in Hesychius and Photius p. 70, 11."}, {"id": "emoris-quote1", "translation": "bereft"}, {"id": "emoris-word1", "translation": "empty, deprived. Aeschylus in Niobe"}, {"id": "emoris-word2", "translation": "bereft"}, {"id": "emoris-word3", "translation": "without share"}, {"id": "en_imperfect-orth1", "translation": "if"}, {"id": "en_imperfect-def1", "translation": "See ἐάν. || ῆν. See εἰμί."}, {"id": "en_imperfect-word1", "translation": "if. || he was"}, {"id": "en_imperfect-word2", "translation": "to be"}, {"id": "enia-orth1", "translation": "reins"}]}